---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import SectionContainer from "@/components/SectionContainer.astro"
import CaseHeader from "@/components/CaseHeader.astro"
import LinkButton from "@/components/LinkButton.astro"
import GitHub from "@/components/icons/GitHub.astro"
---

<Layout
  title="Household Chores — rules in Postgres | Diego Sarmiento"
  description="Case study of Household Chores: a React Native and Supabase mobile app where permissions, points and photo cleanup live in Postgres (RLS, triggers and pg_cron)."
>
  <main class="px-4 py-24">
    <SectionContainer>
      <CaseHeader
        kicker="Postgres · case study"
        title="Household Chores — the rules live in the database"
        summary="A family splits chores, the kids upload a before and an after photo, and they earn points. The interesting part is not the app: a kid cannot cheat even by tampering with the app, because Postgres enforces the rules."
      />

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <LinkButton href="https://github.com/degosenpai211/casita">
          <GitHub class="size-5" />
          Code
        </LinkButton>
      </div>

      <section class="mt-14 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Problem</h2>
        <p>
          The app talks straight to Supabase, with no backend of its own in
          between. Any rule that lives only in the frontend can be skipped by
          calling the API with the kid's token. If the rules matter (who
          touches which chore, who earns points), they have to live in the
          database.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">How it is built</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">8 tables with RLS enabled</strong>
            and 27 policies (3 of them on the photos): each family only sees its own data, and a kid can
            only update the chores assigned to them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">A defense-in-depth trigger:</strong>
            even when the policy lets an UPDATE through, if a kid changes the
            assignee, the points or the due date, the database rejects the row.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Points in a ledger:</strong>
            when a chore is completed, a trigger inserts the entry into
            <code>points_ledger</code> and updates the balance. The client
            never writes points.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">pg_cron for what happens on its own:</strong>
            every 15 minutes it flags overdue chores, and every night it queues
            photos older than 7 days; an Edge Function deletes them from
            Storage, which Postgres cannot touch directly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Offline photos:</strong>
            with no signal, the photo goes into a local queue and uploads by
            itself when the network comes back.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The full schema is ~1,000 lines of commented SQL. The app (Expo
          Router, TypeScript, React Query) has one view for the parent and
          another for the kids.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Trade-offs</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">No backend of its own:</strong>
            less code and fewer servers to run, in exchange for logic written
            in PL/pgSQL, which is harder to test.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Balance cached on the profile:</strong>
            reading it is instant, but it has to stay in sync with the ledger;
            that is why only triggers touch it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Two-step deletion:</strong>
            the database flags and queues, the Edge Function deletes the file.
            More moving parts, but no orphaned photos left in Storage.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">Honest limits</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>
            The "no completion without a before and an after photo" rule is
            enforced in the UI today, not in the database. The next step is a
            <code>CHECK</code> or a trigger that requires it.
          </li>
          <li>There are no automated tests for the RLS policies.</li>
          <li>
            Reward redemption on screen, push notifications and generating
            recurring chores are still missing (the data model is ready).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 space-y-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white">What it shows</h2>
        <p>
          That I can use Postgres beyond SELECT: row-level permissions,
          triggers, scheduled jobs and a points ledger. It is the same kind of
          care a system that handles real money needs.
        </p>
      </section>
    </SectionContainer>
  </main>
</Layout>
